"explanation": "This question relates an observable temperature change to thermodynamic concepts. The system is the chemical reaction occurring in the mixture. The surroundings are everything else, including the thermometer and the solution itself. The observation is that the temperature of the mixture drops. This means the reaction is absorbing heat energy from its surroundings (the solution) to proceed. A process that absorbs heat from its surroundings is defined as an endothermic process. By convention in chemistry, heat absorbed by the system is given a positive sign (q > 0). The enthalpy change (ΔH) for a reaction at constant pressure is equal to the heat flow (q). Therefore, for an endothermic process, ΔH is positive (ΔH > 0). An exothermic reaction, by contrast, releases heat into the surroundings, causing the temperature of the mixture to rise, and has a negative ΔH. So, the process is endothermic, and ΔH > 0. The options 'exothermic reaction with ΔH > 0' and 'endothermic reaction with ΔH < 0' are self-contradictory."
