"answer": "It allows for the migration of ions between the half-cells to maintain charge neutrality.",
"explanation": "A galvanic cell generates electricity from a spontaneous redox reaction. Electrons flow from the anode (where oxidation occurs) to the cathode (where reduction occurs) through an external wire. This flow of negative charge would quickly cause a buildup of positive charge (cations) in the anode compartment and a buildup of negative charge (anions from the electrolyte) in the cathode compartment. This charge imbalance would oppose the further flow of electrons and stop the cell from working very quickly. The purpose of the salt bridge is to prevent this charge buildup and complete the electrical circuit. It contains an inert electrolyte (e.g., KNO₃). Anions (NO₃⁻) from the salt bridge migrate into the anode compartment to neutralize the excess positive charge being built up from the oxidation process. Cations (K⁺) from the salt bridge migrate into the cathode compartment to neutralize the excess negative charge left behind as the cations in the solution are reduced. This flow of ions maintains charge neutrality in both half-cells, allowing the electron flow in the external circuit to continue. It does not allow electrons to flow (that's the wire's job), it does not prevent ion flow, and it does not provide reactants."
